    <section id="what-models">
      <h2>What is an <em>AI model</em>?</h2>
      <p>
        An AI model is a trained software system that learns patterns from examples. Instead of following only
        a fixed set of handwritten rules, it learns from large amounts of data so it can generate text, answer
        questions, summarize, translate, write code, and reason through prompts.
      </p>
      <p>
        A model is not magic, and it is not “thinking” the way a person thinks. It is a highly trained pattern
        system. When you give it new input, it uses what it learned to predict what should come next.
      </p>
      <p>In the pond metaphor:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Learned knowledge is like <b>sediment</b>.</li>
        <li>The current conversation or task is like <b>water</b>.</li>
        <li>Focus and lookback are like the <b>notebook</b> and <b>heads</b>.</li>
        <li>Outside world, tools, and documents are like the <b>surroundings</b>.</li>
        <li>Post-training goals are shaped by the <b>surveyor</b> (reward model) and the <b>sea</b> (checkable outcomes).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="popular">
      <h2>Popular models and <em>model families</em></h2>
      <p>
        Names such as GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Phi, or DeepSeek often refer to
        <b>model families</b>, not one single object. A family can include a base model, instruct/chat versions,
        fine-tunes, quantized packages, and adapters.
      </p>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What people call a “model”</th>
            <th>What it often is</th>
            <th>Pond metaphor</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Hosted frontier models (GPT, Claude, Gemini…)</td>
            <td>Full models via a provider’s API or product</td>
            <td>A remote pond cared for by a provider</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Open-weight families (Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Phi, DeepSeek…)</td>
            <td>Weights you can often download, run, or adapt</td>
            <td>A pond species you can study or host locally</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Base models</td>
            <td>General language patterns</td>
            <td>Deep sediment and raw landscape</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Instruct / chat models</td>
            <td>Refined to follow instructions helpfully</td>
            <td>Banks and surveyors trained for careful navigation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fine-tuned models</td>
            <td>Further trained for a task or domain</td>
            <td>A pond shaped for a particular purpose</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Adapters / LoRA</td>
            <td>Small updates attached to a base model</td>
            <td>Removable lenses or signposts for the lookers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Quantized models</td>
            <td>Compressed for easier local runs</td>
            <td>A smaller, more portable pond</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section id="adapters">
      <h2>Are popular models <em>adapters</em>?</h2>
      <p>
        Most well-known model families are <b>not</b> adapters by themselves. They are full model families or
        hosted systems. An <b>adapter</b> is a smaller piece of training that attaches to an existing base
        model. Instead of rebuilding the whole model, it changes behavior in a focused way.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A <b>model</b> is the whole trained system.</li>
        <li>A <b>fine-tune</b> may update a larger part of that system.</li>
        <li>An <b>adapter</b> is a small add-on that plugs into an existing model.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pond mapping: the <b>base model</b> is landscape and sediment; an <b>adapter</b> adds signposts or lenses
        that guide lookback and style — it does not dig a whole new pond from scratch. Glossary:
        <a href={withBase("glossary.html#adapter")}>LoRA / adapter</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="huggingface">
      <h2>What is <em>Hugging Face</em>?</h2>
      <p>
        Hugging Face is a platform and ecosystem where people find, share, train, evaluate, and run AI models —
        often described as a community hub for machine learning. It can host full weights, base and instruct
        models, fine-tunes, adapters/LoRA, tokenizers, datasets, evaluation tools, and model cards.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the pond metaphor, Hugging Face is a <b>nursery, library, and field guide</b> for ponds: discover
        species, read what each is good at, download weights or adapters, compare families, and find data for
        training or evaluation. It does not replace the pond; it helps you find and bring one into your
        environment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ollama">
      <h2>What is <em>Ollama</em>?</h2>
      <p>
        Ollama helps you run and manage AI models on your own computer — download, run, and interact through
        simple commands or local interfaces. It is mainly a <b>runtime</b> and <b>model manager</b>, not primarily
        a training hub.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the pond metaphor, Ollama is a <b>local pond keeper</b>: choose a model, bring it onto your machine,
        run it, chat or connect apps, manage versions, and experiment more privately. If Hugging Face is where
        you discover seeds and blueprints, Ollama is one way to set up a local pond and watch it run.
      </p>
      <h3>How they fit together</h3>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tool / concept</th>
            <th>Role in AI</th>
            <th>Role at the pond</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Hugging Face</td>
            <td>Hub, library, datasets, adapters</td>
            <td>Nursery, field guide, model library</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ollama</td>
            <td>Local runtime and manager</td>
            <td>Local pond keeper</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Base model</td>
            <td>General patterns</td>
            <td>Sediment and landscape</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Adapter</td>
            <td>Specialized update</td>
            <td>Signposts / lenses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Prompt</td>
            <td>User input</td>
            <td>A ripple in the water</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Context window</td>
            <td>Active information</td>
            <td>Visible water around the looker</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Attention</td>
            <td>What matters</td>
            <td>Notebook / heads looking across the pond</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grounding</td>
            <td>External facts / tools</td>
            <td>Surroundings</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section id="made">
      <h2>How models are <em>made</em></h2>
      <p>Models are shaped through stages — not created all at once:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Design</b> — architecture, size, learning approach.</li>
        <li><b>Data preparation</b> — collect and clean examples.</li>
        <li><b>Tokenization</b> — break language into processable pieces.</li>
        <li><b>Pre-training</b> — learn broad patterns (sediment forms).</li>
        <li><b>Post-training</b> — become more helpful and instruction-following (banks / surveyor / sea).</li>
        <li><b>Fine-tuning or adapters</b> — specialize for tasks or domains.</li>
        <li><b>Evaluation</b> — strengths, weaknesses, failure modes.</li>
        <li><b>Deployment</b> — APIs, products, or local runtimes such as Ollama.</li>
        <li><b>Feedback and improvement</b> — real use reveals where to improve.</li>
      </ol>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Stage</th>
            <th>Pond metaphor</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Design</td><td>Decide what kind of pond ecosystem to build</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data preparation</td><td>Gather material that will settle into the pond</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tokenization</td><td>Turn inputs into ripples the pond can respond to</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pre-training</td><td>Sediment forms over time</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Post-training</td><td>Surveyors and seas shape helpful banks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fine-tuning / adapters</td><td>Signposts and lenses for specific behavior</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Check the health of the pond</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Open the pond or run it locally</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Feedback</td><td>New ripples reveal how the ecosystem responds</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section id="architectures">
      <h2>Model architectures: different <em>pond designs</em></h2>
      <p>
        Not every AI model is built the same way. An <b>architecture</b> is the design plan for how a model
        processes information: how it stores what it has seen, how it looks back at context, and how fast it
        can respond. In pond terms: how the water flows, who looks at the pond, and how much can be seen at once.
      </p>
      <p>Four friendly families (a map, not a permanent rulebook):</p>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Architecture</th>
            <th>What it is</th>
            <th>Popular examples</th>
            <th>Pond metaphor</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Transformer (dense)</td>
            <td>Tokens look back via attention</td>
            <td>GPT family, Claude, Llama 3, Mistral, Phi, Qwen (dense), early Gemini</td>
            <td>A looker who can instantly scan any point on the water</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)</td>
            <td>Many specialists; few activate per token</td>
            <td>Mixtral, DeepSeek-V3/R1, Qwen-MoE, Grok, Llama 4, Gemini 1.5 Pro (reported)</td>
            <td>A team of specialists — only the right ones wake for each ripple</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SSM / Mamba-style</td>
            <td>Streaming with compact hidden state</td>
            <td>Mamba, Mamba-2, Cartesia Sonic, Falcon-H1</td>
            <td>A flowing stream: the water carries memory at constant depth</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hybrid (SSM + attention)</td>
            <td>Streaming layers plus attention where needed</td>
            <td>Jamba, Nemotron-H, Zamba, Bamba, LFM2, Qwen3-Next</td>
            <td>A current that flows — with lookers who glance back at key moments</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="arch-card highlight" style="margin-top:16px">
        <h3>The architecture this demo shows: Hybrid</h3>
        <p class="one-line">PondScope visualizes hybrid, SSM-style models — flowing water plus lookback.</p>
        <p>
          <b>Water</b> is the SSM side: a compact state that streams forward. <b>Notebook / heads</b> are the
          attention side: focused lookback when precise recall matters. That is why Labs
          <a href={withBase("pond-continuous.html")}>01</a> and
          <a href={withBase("multi-head.html")}>03</a> pair so well — and why the
          <a href={withBase("issues-lab.html")}>Issues Lab</a> can show trade-offs under stress.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="arch-grid">
        <article class="arch-card">
          <h3>Transformer (dense attention)</h3>
          <p class="one-line">The looker can scan anywhere on the water, instantly.</p>
          <div class="lists">
            <div>
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Excellent associative recall across context</li>
                <li>Training parallelizes well at scale</li>
                <li>Largest ecosystem (tools, adapters, research)</li>
                <li>Attention maps aid debugging</li>
                <li>Proven quality across task sizes</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Prefill cost grows quickly with length</li>
                <li>KV cache grows with the conversation</li>
                <li>TTFT slows on long prompts</li>
                <li>Less efficient for very long streams</li>
                <li>Harder long-context on edge hardware</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="arch-card">
          <h3>Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)</h3>
          <p class="one-line">A team of specialists; only the right ones wake for each ripple.</p>
          <div class="lists">
            <div>
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Large capacity with less compute per token</li>
                <li>Often faster than a dense model of same size</li>
                <li>Strong quality-per-cost at frontier scale</li>
                <li>Experts can specialize (code, languages…)</li>
                <li>Parameters can grow without proportional training cost</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>All experts must be stored — large footprint</li>
                <li>Often memory-bandwidth limited</li>
                <li>Trickier training (routing, balance)</li>
                <li>Fine-tunes/adapters more complex</li>
                <li>Distributed serving adds latency variance</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="arch-card">
          <h3>State Space Model (SSM / Mamba)</h3>
          <p class="one-line">A flowing stream — the water itself carries memory forward.</p>
          <div class="lists">
            <div>
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Cost grows linearly with length</li>
                <li>Fixed-size state — no growing KV cache</li>
                <li>Steady streaming, low per-token latency</li>
                <li>Predictable memory for long documents</li>
                <li>Suited to on-device / edge deployment</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Weaker at precise long-range copy/recall</li>
                <li>Fixed state compresses fine details</li>
                <li>Smaller ecosystem than Transformers</li>
                <li>Less interpretability tooling</li>
                <li>Thinner frontier-scale track record</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="arch-card">
          <h3>Hybrid (SSM + attention)</h3>
          <p class="one-line">A stream that flows — with lookers who glance up at the right moments.</p>
          <div class="lists">
            <div>
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>Efficient streaming plus precise recall</li>
                <li>Better long-context recall than pure SSMs</li>
                <li>Smaller KV cache than pure Transformers</li>
                <li>Strong efficiency–quality balance</li>
                <li>Attention can be placed where needed</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ol>
                <li>More design knobs (layer ratios)</li>
                <li>Harder to implement and serve</li>
                <li>Tooling still maturing</li>
                <li>Harder to attribute behavior when debugging</li>
                <li>Fewer public training recipes</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="enrich-note" style="margin-top:14px">
        <b>Issues Lab tie-in:</b> each architecture handles stress differently. Watch how token bloat, latency,
        and recall show up — then connect them to these designs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="broader-designs">
      <h2>Core &amp; specialized designs: <em>other pond species</em></h2>
      <p>
        The section above focuses on <b>sequence / language memory designs</b> (what PondScope’s labs
        visualize). AI also uses other architectures for vision, generation, compression, and mixed media.
        These are <b>additive</b> — they do not replace Transformers, MoE, SSMs, or hybrids. Modern systems
        often <b>compose</b> several of them.
      </p>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Design</th>
            <th>What it is</th>
            <th>Typical uses</th>
            <th>Pond metaphor</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)</td>
            <td>Spatial filters that slide over grid-like data</td>
            <td>Computer vision, image recognition, video frames</td>
            <td>Reading a surface with local ripples — patches of shoreline, not a long token stream</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs / LSTMs)</td>
            <td>Internal loops process sequences step-by-step with a hidden state</td>
            <td>Classic time-series and language modeling; ancestor of modern state models</td>
            <td>
              Early “water carries memory forward” — closest ancestor of
              <a href={withBase("pond-continuous.html")}>Lab 01 · Pond</a> / SSM-style flow
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)</td>
            <td>A generator and a discriminator compete</td>
            <td>Synthesizing images, video, and other media</td>
            <td>Two ponds in a contest: one invents shorescapes; the other judges whether they look real</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Autoencoders</td>
            <td>Compress input through a bottleneck, then reconstruct</td>
            <td>Compression, denoising, anomaly detection</td>
            <td>Squeeze the landscape into a deep well of features, then rebuild the shore from that well</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)</td>
            <td>Routes work to specialized subnetworks</td>
            <td>Large language models with sparse activation</td>
            <td>
              Already covered above — see
              <a href="#architectures">Architectures · MoE</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Multimodal systems</td>
            <td>Separate encoders (or shared stacks) for text, image, audio, video</td>
            <td>Captioning, vision-language models, cross-modal search</td>
            <td>Several inlets into one ecosystem — surroundings and sediment from different senses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Diffusion models</td>
            <td>Learn to reverse a stepwise process of adding noise</td>
            <td>High-quality image and media generation</td>
            <td>Clearing murky water step by step until a clear scene appears</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <b>How this relates to PondScope:</b> Labs 01–03 are about <b>runtime sequence memory</b> (pond water,
        notebook, heads). CNNs, GANs, autoencoders, and diffusion are different species — still neural networks
        with weights (sediment), still trained and evaluated, but not what the interactive twin is simulating.
        RNNs/LSTMs are the historical bridge: fixed-size evolving state → today’s SSM/pond story.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="weights">
      <h2>Weights: <em>what the sediment is made of</em></h2>
      <p>
        <b>Weights</b> (also called parameters) are the learned numbers inside a neural network — the result of
        training. In PondScope terms, they are the <b>sediment</b>: the lasting knowledge shaped during
        pre-training and later post-training or fine-tuning. When you run a model, the weights stay mostly
        fixed while <b>water</b> (activations / context) moves.
      </p>
      <p>
        A modern language model may have billions of weights. Together with the architecture (how those weights
        are wired), they define the model’s capabilities. An <b>adapter</b> or LoRA adds a small set of extra
        weights on top of a base — see <a href="#adapters">Adapters</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Closed weights (proprietary)</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Closed weights</b> means you do not receive the parameter files. You typically access the model only
        through a provider’s product or API. You can send prompts and get outputs, but you cannot download the
        full sediment, freely inspect every parameter, or run the exact same checkpoint offline without the
        provider’s permission and infrastructure.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Typical use:</b> call an API, use a hosted chat product, or an agent that routes to that API.</li>
        <li>
          <b>What you can usually do:</b> prompt, tool-use via the product, sometimes fine-tune through a
          provider-managed service, evaluate via API outputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>What you usually cannot do:</b> host the full model yourself, deeply modify base weights, or
          redistribute the checkpoint.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Pond metaphor:</b> a well-maintained <b>remote pond</b> — you visit and interact; you don’t take
          the sediment home.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Open weights</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Open weights</b> means the parameter files (the sediment) are published so you can download them —
        often from hubs like <a href="#huggingface">Hugging Face</a>. Licenses still matter: “open weights” is
        not the same as fully open-source code, training data, and unrestricted commercial use. Always read the
        model card and license.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Typical use:</b> download → run locally (e.g. <a href="#ollama">Ollama</a> or other runtimes) or
          on your own servers; fine-tune or attach adapters; quantize for smaller machines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>What you can usually do:</b> offline/private inference, custom serving, research, evaluation on
          your hardware, domain adapters, experimentation with decoding and tooling.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Limits:</b> you still need compute, software stacks, and compliance with the license; quality and
          safety are your responsibility when you host.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Pond metaphor:</b> a pond species you can <b>study, host, or adapt locally</b> — nursery at Hugging
          Face, local keeper at Ollama.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Open weights vs “open source”</h3>
      <p>
        People often blur these terms. <b>Open weights</b> = downloadable parameters.
        <b>Open source</b> (in the strict software sense) usually means source code under an OSI-approved
        license. A model can have open weights with a restrictive license, or release weights without releasing
        training code or datasets. Treat each artifact separately: weights, code, data, and license.
      </p>

      <h3>How you use weights — and what you get</h3>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Action</th>
            <th>Closed weights</th>
            <th>Open weights</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Chat / complete</td>
            <td>Via API or product</td>
            <td>Locally or self-hosted, or via someone else’s hosting of that checkpoint</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fine-tune / adapters</td>
            <td>Only if the provider offers it</td>
            <td>On your hardware or cloud GPUs; LoRA common</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Quantize / compress</td>
            <td>Usually not your choice</td>
            <td>Common for laptops and edge (smaller portable pond)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Evaluate</td>
            <td>Black-box: score outputs you can call</td>
            <td>Black-box and white-box options; full control of harness</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Build agents</td>
            <td>Agent products call the API</td>
            <td>Agent stack can point at local or self-hosted endpoints</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Redistribute the checkpoint</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>Only if the license allows</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section id="evals">
      <h2>Evals &amp; benchmarks: <em>checking the health of the pond</em></h2>
      <p>
        Training and clever demos are not enough. <b>Evaluation</b> is how we measure whether a model (or an
        adapter, agent, or product) actually does what we claim — and where it fails. In pond terms: you check
        the health of the ecosystem before you trust it with visitors.
      </p>

      <h3>What evals are vs what benchmarks are</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          An <b>eval</b> (evaluation) is any systematic test: a task, a metric, a procedure, and a way to
          score outputs. Evals can be public or private, automated or human-judged, narrow or broad.
        </li>
        <li>
          A <b>benchmark</b> is a shared, named eval suite that the community uses to compare systems — fixed
          (or versioned) datasets and scoring so scores are somewhat comparable across models and papers.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Benchmarks are a kind of eval. Not every eval is a famous benchmark: your own holdout set for a medical
        FAQ bot is an eval; MMLU or a coding suite is a benchmark many people recognize.
      </p>

      <h3>Why evals matter</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Claims need evidence</b> — “better at coding” should mean measured improvement, not vibes.</li>
        <li><b>Catch regressions</b> — a new fine-tune can help one task and hurt another.</li>
        <li><b>Compare options</b> — closed API vs open weights vs adapter A vs adapter B.</li>
        <li><b>Safety and reliability</b> — toxicity, jailbreaks, hallucination, and grounding failures show up in targeted evals.</li>
        <li><b>Cost and latency</b> — quality is not the only scoreboard; TTFT and $/1K tokens matter in production.</li>
        <li>
          <b>Honest teaching</b> — PondScope’s Issues Lab and your own holdouts exist so failure modes stay
          visible, not hidden behind a single happy demo.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What evals do (what they measure)</h3>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Kind of signal</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
            <th>What you learn</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Knowledge / QA</td>
            <td>Multi-task academic suites, domain quizzes</td>
            <td>Whether sediment covers the facts you care about</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reasoning</td>
            <td>Math, logic, multi-step problems</td>
            <td>Whether the model can chain steps, not only memorize</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Coding</td>
            <td>HumanEval-style, repo bugs, agent tool tasks</td>
            <td>Whether code and tool use work under test harnesses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Instruction following</td>
            <td>Format, constraints, refusals</td>
            <td>Whether post-training / surveyor shaping stuck</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grounding / RAG</td>
            <td>Answer-from-docs, citation checks</td>
            <td>Whether surroundings are used correctly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Safety</td>
            <td>Harmful content, bias probes</td>
            <td>Where the banks fail under adversarial ripples</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Systems metrics</td>
            <td>Latency, TTFT, throughput, cost, memory</td>
            <td>Whether the pond is usable at your scale</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>How to run evals (practical paths)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <b>Define the question</b> — What decision will the score support? (Ship adapter? Pick a model?
          Detect token-bloat sensitivity?)
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Choose or build a task set</b> — Use a public benchmark when comparability matters; use a
          <b>private holdout</b> when you need honesty about your domain (avoid training on the test).
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Fix the protocol</b> — prompts, decoding settings (temperature), tool access, number of trials,
          and scoring rules. Changing these silently breaks comparisons.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Run the harness</b> — scripts or frameworks that call the model (API or local), collect outputs,
          and compute metrics. For open weights, run on your stack; for closed weights, evaluate through the API.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Score</b> — exact match, unit tests for code, rubric/LLM-as-judge (with care), or human review for
          nuanced tasks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Analyze errors</b> — the value is often in <em>which</em> items failed (grounding? length? format?),
          not only the headline number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Gate decisions</b> — e.g. “adapter must beat baseline on holdout and not collapse general probes”
          — ship only if the gate passes.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>What you get from evals</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Scores and comparisons</b> — numbers (and confidence intervals when you have enough samples).</li>
        <li><b>Error reports</b> — concrete failure examples to fix data, prompts, or training.</li>
        <li><b>Go / no-go signals</b> — whether to deploy, rollback, or keep iterating.</li>
        <li><b>Trade-off maps</b> — quality vs latency vs cost; closed API vs local open weights.</li>
        <li>
          <b>Humility</b> — benchmarks can be contaminated, gamed, or too narrow. A high public score does not
          guarantee your users’ tasks work. Pair leaderboards with <b>your</b> holdouts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="enrich-note">
        <b>PondScope link:</b> the Issues Lab is a teaching eval for failure modes (token bloat, latency,
        grounding) — visual, not a leaderboard. Production teams use the same spirit with automated harnesses
        and held-out sets. See also <a href={withBase("issues-lab.html")}>Issues Lab</a> and
        <a href="#behave">How models behave</a>.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="behave">
      <h2>How models <em>behave</em></h2>
      <p>
        Once made, a model still behaves in particular ways. It works with patterns, context, attention, and
        probabilities — and can become overloaded or lose grounding.
      </p>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Behavior</th>
            <th>What it means</th>
            <th>Pond metaphor</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Next-token prediction</td>
            <td>Predicts likely next pieces of text</td>
            <td>Ripples moving across the water</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Context window</td>
            <td>Active information the model can use</td>
            <td>Visible water around the looker</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Attention</td>
            <td>Deciding which parts matter</td>
            <td>Notebook / heads focusing on areas</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory</td>
            <td>Learned knowledge from training</td>
            <td>Sediment</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Token bloat</td>
            <td>Too much active context</td>
            <td>Crowded or slow water</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Latency / TTFT</td>
            <td>Delay before responding</td>
            <td>The looker takes longer to act</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grounding</td>
            <td>Connection to reliable outside info</td>
            <td>Surroundings</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hallucination</td>
            <td>Confident but unreliable output</td>
            <td>Misreading the pond</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Explore directly:
        <a href={withBase("pond-continuous.html")}>pond</a> ·
        <a href={withBase("pond-vs-attention.html")}>notebook</a> ·
        <a href={withBase("multi-head.html")}>heads</a> ·
        <a href={withBase("issues-lab.html")}>Issues Lab</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="agents">
      <h2>Coding agents: <em>helpers at the workbench</em></h2>
      <p>
        A <b>coding agent</b> is software that uses one or more models to help you write, edit, explain, and
        ship code — often with tools (file edit, terminal, search, browsers) and a loop of plan → act → check.
        Agents are not a separate kind of “brain”; they are products and workflows that <b>orchestrate</b> models.
      </p>
      <p>In the pond metaphor:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>model</b> is the pond (sediment + water + lookback).</li>
        <li>The <b>agent product</b> is the workbench and tools around the pond.</li>
        <li>Your <b>prompt / task</b> is a ripple that may trigger many tool steps.</li>
        <li><b>Repo context</b> is surroundings the agent can ground in (files, diffs, docs).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Types of coding agents (friendly map)</h3>
      <table class="prose-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>What it emphasizes</th>
            <th>Examples (illustrative)</th>
            <th>Pond role</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>IDE-native agent</td>
            <td>Edits your project inside an editor, with repo awareness</td>
            <td>Cursor, GitHub Copilot Chat / agent modes, Windsurf</td>
            <td>Workbench built into the boathouse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>App / UI builders</td>
            <td>Generate apps from prompts; less raw-repo focus</td>
            <td>Lovable, v0-style builders, Bolt-like tools</td>
            <td>Prefabricated docks — fast shorelines</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CLI / autonomous loops</td>
            <td>Long-running tool use from a terminal or cloud VM</td>
            <td>Claude Code-style CLIs, cloud agents, Aider-like flows</td>
            <td>Remote keepers walking the banks with tools</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Chat assistants with code</td>
            <td>Conversation-first; paste/snippets more than deep repo loops</td>
            <td>ChatGPT / Claude / Gemini web apps in coding mode</td>
            <td>Visiting a well-kept remote pond</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Local / open stacks</td>
            <td>Run open models via Ollama or similar; more privacy control</td>
            <td>Continue.dev + Ollama, local Open WebUI setups</td>
            <td>Your own pond keeper + local habitat</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Cursor, Lovable, and model choice</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Cursor</b> is an IDE-native coding agent: it pairs an editor with agent features (chat, inline edit,
        multi-file changes, tools). You typically choose a <b>frontier hosted model</b> (or a team-configured
        option) for planning and edits; the product supplies the workbench, context assembly, and apply loop.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Lovable</b> (and similar builders) leans toward <b>product generation</b>: describe an app, get UI and
        scaffolding quickly. The model is still a next-token predictor under the hood, but the agent’s job is
        scoped to shipping a front-end experience rather than deep surgery on an arbitrary legacy repo.
      </p>
      <p>What “model types” agents offer usually means one of:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Hosted frontier chat/instruct models</b> — strong general coding via provider APIs.</li>
        <li><b>Fast / cheaper models</b> — for autocomplete, small edits, or high-volume steps.</li>
        <li><b>Reasoning-oriented variants</b> — more deliberation for hard debugging (when offered).</li>
        <li><b>Bring-your-own / local models</b> — open weights through Ollama or compatible endpoints (varies by product).</li>
        <li><b>Specialized coding fine-tunes</b> — models tuned for code completion or agent tool use.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Agents differ in <b>how</b> they offer models: picker in settings, automatic routing (fast vs strong),
        org-locked models, or local endpoints. The pond stays the same idea — sediment + water + lookback —
        while the agent changes which tools touch the surroundings (your files).
      </p>
      <div class="enrich-note">
        PondScope itself was built with agentic IDE help (Cursor · Grok 4.5) after the metaphor was named in a
        Claude conversation — see <a href={withBase("context.html")}>Context</a>. That is an example of an
        IDE-native agent loop around a hybrid teaching metaphor, not a claim about any vendor’s internal stack.
      </div>
